  completeExplanation: 'Plants are primary producers that sustain Earth\'s ecosystems by converting abiotic solar energy into bioavailable chemical energy. Through photosynthesis, green leaf mesophyll cells utilize chlorophyll to trap photon energy, splitting water and combining it with atmospheric carbon dioxide absorbed via microscopic stomata to synthesize glucose (C6H12O6) and release molecular oxygen (O2). Because glucose is instantly metabolized or water-soluble, plants polymerize excess sugar into insoluble starch granules for compact storage. Decolourizing leaves in boiling alcohol removes green chlorophyll pigments, allowing clear visualization of the deep blue-black iodine-starch complex. Gas exchange is regulated by paired kidney-shaped guard cells flanking each stoma. Vascular circulation is mediated by two parallel systems: xylem, composed of hollow dead tracheary elements conducting water and minerals unidirectionally upwards driven by transpiration pull; and phloem, composed of living sieve elements translocating photosynthates bidirectionally to growing shoot apices and subterranean storage organs. In addition to food synthesis, all living plant cells continuously undergo cellular respiration day and night, consuming oxygen to oxidize glucose and generate cellular ATP while evolving carbon dioxide and water. Non-green or ecologically specialized flora utilize alternate nutritional strategies: Cuscuta (Amarbel) is an obligate parasite employing haustoria to tap host vascular bundles; insectivorous plants like Nepenthes digest captured insects to extract nitrogen in acidic bogs; fungi act as saprotrophs by external enzyme secretion; and symbiotic mutualisms (Lichens combining algae and fungi, Rhizobium bacteria in legume root nodules) allow cooperative sharing of photosynthates and fixed atmospheric nitrogen.',
